Dear Editors,

#v(0.4em)
Please consider the transferred manuscript *Fragile Authentication of PDF Signatures via Bit-Exact Quantization Index Modulation on Vector Geometry* (transferred from *Signal Processing*, manuscript SIGPRO-D-26-04464) for publication in *Digital Signal Processing*.

#v(0.4em)
*Why this journal.* The paper is a watermarking/signal-processing study, not a cryptography protocol paper: it asks how a fragile payload can be carried by the geometry of a visible mark, how exact (bit-for-bit) recovery can be achieved with dithered quantization index modulation, and what capacity–distortion trade-off results on a fixed 27-degree-of-freedom carrier. Those are core DSP questions, and the manuscript is now written for that readership.

#v(0.4em)
*What changed on transfer.* The transferred file was the earlier submission. Before completing this submission I have revised it substantially: a new title and abstract framed for the DSP/watermarking readership; a rebuilt, verified bibliography (every citation resolves to a DOI, standard or book — the earlier draft contained references that could not be verified, and they were removed); a new PDF-level evaluation on a 180-file corpus from three document generators (LibreOffice, Typst, Chromium print) in native, flattened and print-scan-simulated variants, with a carrier ablation and same-carrier baselines; and the optical/screen-photo and API material moved out of this paper into a companion manuscript.

#v(0.4em)
*What the paper claims and does not claim.* It reports exact recovery of a 266-bit payload, fragility to coordinate edits and re-serialisation, fail-closed behaviour under flattening, and a capacity–distortion comparison in which capacity-limited baselines are reported as such. It does not claim cryptographic security from a watermarking argument, and it explicitly complements rather than replaces PAdES/eIDAS signatures.

#v(0.4em)
*Declarations.* The manuscript is not under consideration elsewhere; the preprint is archived on Zenodo (concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23429017) and SSRN (10.2139/ssrn.7631458); a Ukrainian patent application (a 2026 06266) covers the sealing method. The evaluation harness and corpus manifest are available for reproducibility.

#v(0.8em)
Thank you for considering the transfer.

#v(0.8em)
Sincerely, \
Yaroslav V. Baienko
